{
  "dom_id": "step-q103852",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let $f(x)=(1+x)^{3/2}$ and let ${P}_{2}(x)$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(x)$ is used to approximate $(1.9)^{3/2}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-0{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $x=0.9.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.9$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}|0.9-0{|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.9{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0,0.9].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1+x)^{3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}(1+x)^{1/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}(1+x)^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|-\\frac{3}{8}(1+x)^{-3/2}|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+x)^{3/2}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.9],$ its value is greatest at $x=0.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(0)|=\\frac{3}{8(1+0)^{3/2}}=\\frac{3}{8},\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{6}|0.9{|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.9)^{3}}{16} \\\\  & ≈0.046.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
